<h1 class="wp-block-post-title">iPhone 18 Pro Camera: Depth of Field and Depth of Proof</h1>

	<div class="article-sub-title">
		<p>With a physical aperture and cryptographically signed Reference Images, Apple rethinks both how a phone photograph looks and how it proves itself real</p>
	</div>
	


<div class="wp-block-group article-meta is-layout-flow wp-container-core-group-is-layout-9f35e848 wp-block-group-is-layout-flow"><div class="wp-block-post-author-name"><a href="https://coolhunting.com/author/josh/" target="_self" class="wp-block-post-author-name__link">Josh Rubin</a></div>

<div class="wp-block-post-date"><time datetime="2026-09-16T08:00:00-04:00">16 September 2026</time></div></div>


<figure class="alignfull wp-block-post-featured-image"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="572" src="https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-1024x572.jpeg" class="attachment-large size-large wp-post-image" alt="iPhone 18 Pro Main Camera Aperture" style="object-fit:cover;" srcset="https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-1024x572.jpeg 1024w, https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-300x168.jpeg 300w, https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-768x429.jpeg 768w, https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-1536x859.jpeg 1536w, https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-2048x1145.jpeg 2048w, https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-600x335.jpeg 600w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" data-attachment-id="448968" data-permalink="https://coolhunting.com/sc-4/" data-orig-file="https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-scaled.jpeg" data-orig-size="2560,1431" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="iPhone 18 Pro Main Camera Aperture" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;Courtesy of Apple&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://coolhunting.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-main-camera-aperture-1024x572.jpeg" /></figure></header>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With the <a href="http://go.redirectingat.com/?id=77721X1528342&amp;xs=1&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.apple.com%2Fiphone-18-pro%2F&amp;sref=rss">iPhone 18 Pro</a> and <a href="http://go.redirectingat.com/?id=77721X1528342&amp;xs=1&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.apple.com%2Fiphone-18-pro%2F&amp;sref=rss">Pro Max</a>, <a href="https://coolhunting.com/?s=Apple">Apple</a> has fitted one of its cameras with a mechanical variable aperture for the first time. Six laser-cut blades on the new 48MP Fusion Main camera move between f/1.48, f/1.8, f/2.8 and f/4.0. Apple says each blade is thinner than a human hair. The setting can be left to iOS or set by hand through a new Pro controls interface that also offers shutter speed, white balance and a histogram. Apple has also added Reference Image, an opt-in mode that preserves a signed and unalterable record of what the sensor saw, along with the 2nm A20 Pro chip, a smaller Dynamic Island and longer battery life. The result is a phone camera that is one step closer to handling light the way a big camera does. We&#8217;ve been shooting with the new Pro cameras since last week&#8217;s announcement, and their evolution is impressive. Putting moving parts inside a phone lens marks a genuine turning point, and in daily use the benefits are clear. The Pro starts at $1,199 and the Pro Max at $1,299, and both arrive 18 September 2026.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"></p>



		<figure class="wp-block-jetpack-videopress jetpack-videopress-player alignwide" style="" >
			<div class="jetpack-videopress-player__wrapper"> <iframe title="VideoPress Video Player" aria-label="VideoPress Video Player" width="500" height="281" src="https://videopress.com/embed/INrlfOlS?cover=1&amp;autoPlay=0&amp;controls=1&amp;loop=0&amp;muted=0&amp;persistVolume=1&amp;playsinline=0&amp;preloadContent=metadata&amp;useAverageColor=1&amp;posterUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoolhunting.com%2Fwp-content%2Fuploads%2F2026%2F09%2Fapple-iphone-18-pro-variable-aperture-h_mov_avc_240p.original.jpg&amp;hd=0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen data-resize-to-parent="true" allow="clipboard-write; presentation"></iframe><script src='https://v0.wordpress.com/js/next/videopress-iframe.js?m=1770107250'></script></div>
			<figcaption>Courtesy of Apple</figcaption>
			
		</figure>
		


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Most people will never touch the aperture setting, though, and Apple has designed for that. In dim rooms the lens opens to f/1.48, wider than the fixed f/1.78 of the <a href="https://coolhunting.com/?s=iPhone+17+Pro">iPhone 17 Pro</a>. With a moving subject in moderate light it also opens wide so the shutter can freeze the motion, and for a group of people at staggered distances it closes toward f/4 to keep everyone sharp. The metadata on our test frames showed the aperture shifting from shot to shot as the camera read each scene.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"></p>



		<figure class="wp-block-jetpack-videopress jetpack-videopress-player alignwide" style="" >
			<div class="jetpack-videopress-player__wrapper"> <iframe title="VideoPress Video Player" aria-label="VideoPress Video Player" width="500" height="281" src="https://videopress.com/embed/AF0YW474?cover=1&amp;autoPlay=0&amp;controls=1&amp;loop=0&amp;muted=0&amp;persistVolume=1&amp;playsinline=0&amp;preloadContent=metadata&amp;useAverageColor=1&amp;posterUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoolhunting.com%2Fwp-content%2Fuploads%2F2026%2F09%2Fapple-iphone-18-pro-pro-controls-h_mov_hd.original.jpg&amp;hd=0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen data-resize-to-parent="true" allow="clipboard-write; presentation"></iframe><script src='https://v0.wordpress.com/js/next/videopress-iframe.js?m=1770107250'></script></div>
			<figcaption>Courtesy of Apple</figcaption>
			
		</figure>
		


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Pro controls hand exposure decisions back to the photographer. The configurable row above the viewfinder, familiar from toggling flash or Live Photos, can now hold live icons showing current f-stop and shutter speed, and tapping either opens a controller along the bottom of the frame. Shutter speed is adjustable in the native Camera app for the first time, with exposures up to a full second. ISO always sets itself, so fixing aperture or shutter prompts the other value and ISO to compensate, while setting both by hand gives more creative control. These functions all apply to video as well.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">App developers get more latitude still. Apple&#8217;s APIs give third-party apps manual control over all three sides of the exposure triangle—aperture, shutter speed and ISO—along with the automatic aperture behavior and intermediate stops anywhere between f/1.48 and f/4.0. Blackmagic Camera had early access and is among the first adopters, with a focus on video.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Stopping down not only reduces the amount of light hitting the sensor and increases the range of what’s in focus, it also changes how points of light render. Light diffracting around the six-blade iris turns streetlamps and glinting earrings into six-pointed stars at f/4.0, an optical effect the fixed round openings of earlier iPhones couldn&#8217;t produce.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"></p>



		<figure class="wp-block-jetpack-videopress jetpack-videopress-player alignwide" style="" >
			<div class="jetpack-videopress-player__wrapper"> <iframe loading="lazy" title="VideoPress Video Player" aria-label="VideoPress Video Player" width="500" height="281" src="https://videopress.com/embed/eGcbVv2S?cover=1&amp;autoPlay=0&amp;controls=1&amp;loop=0&amp;muted=0&amp;persistVolume=1&amp;playsinline=0&amp;preloadContent=metadata&amp;useAverageColor=1&amp;posterUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoolhunting.com%2Fwp-content%2Fuploads%2F2026%2F09%2Fapple-iphone-18-pro-photographic-styles-texture-h_mov_avc_240p.original.jpg&amp;hd=0" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen data-resize-to-parent="true" allow="clipboard-write; presentation"></iframe><script src='https://v0.wordpress.com/js/next/videopress-iframe.js?m=1770107250'></script></div>
			<figcaption>Courtesy of Apple</figcaption>
			
		</figure>
		


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Photographic Styles now add texture controls for skin, glow and film grain. The system applies them semantically, treating skin differently from clothing or background, and every adjustment stays non-destructive so it can be reversed at any time. The grain pattern is Apple&#8217;s own. Its engineers studied how the dye clouds in developed color film clump together, and as in film, the grain gathers in the midtones and thins out in highlights and shadows—a behavior we noticed in our edits before Apple explained it. Texture controls are exclusive to iPhone 18 Pro.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Every main-camera video clip now records depth, so Cinematic focus effects can be applied afterward to footage shot at up to 60 fps. Audio Mix now isolates music as well as voices, and Time-lapse records in 4K Dolby Vision HDR with an adaptive mode that lingers on action and speeds through the lulls.</p>



